1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 a6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Nb3 Bb4 7. Bd3 Bxc3+ 8.
bxc3 d6 ({There seems no reason for not playing} 8... d5 {here}) 9. O-O Be6 10.
f3 Qc7 11. Ba3 O-O 12. c4 b6 {White threatened to rid himself of his weak Pawn
by 13.c5.} ({If} 12... Bxc4 13. Bxd6) 13. f4 Rd8 14. Qe2 exf4 $6 {Creates all
sorts of chances for both sides.} 15. Rxf4 Nc6 ({Now I threaten 16...d5. Not} 
15... d5 16. e5) 16. Raf1 Ne5 {A useful move. White now overestimates his
position and tries for a big attack.} 17. Rh4 $2 {His attack isn't worth the
exchange.} Ng6 18. Rxf6 Nxh4 19. Qh5 $6 Ng6 (19... gxf6 {is playable but, as
I'm winning anyway, why take any risks?}) 20. Bb2 Bxc4 {Better than 20...gxf6
as I get the queens off now.} 21. Rf3 Bxb3 22. axb3 Qc5+ {It's just a matter
of technique now.} 23. Qxc5 dxc5 24. Bc4 Ra7 25. e5 b5 26. Bd3 Nf8 27. Rh3 g6
28. Rg3 Ne6 29. h4 Nf4 30. Bf1 Rd2 31. Bc1 Ne2+ 32. Bxe2 Rxe2 33. Be3 Rxc2 34.
h5 Rc3 35. hxg6 hxg6 36. Rh3 Rxb3 37. Bxc5 Rxh3 {Result of S.M.T +4 =1 -6. I
played well below form throughout. This was my worst tournament result ever.
Source: Dick Collins scorebook, contributed by Sean Parker, 19 December 2022.}
0-1

